WEBVTT

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.620
Journey across the globe from Scotland to China

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.940
with world's greatest train ride videos.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.640
Experience the breathtaking thrill and adventure

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.700
of authentic train travel as you follow the tracks

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.040
to unique history, fascinating people,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.990
and breathtaking scenery.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.240
All aboard.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:29.600
Canada.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:35.210
From the Atlantic to the Pacific, this vast nation, one of the largest of the world, encompasses

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.810
towering mountains, rolling prairies, the great lakes, and crashing shores.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:46.440
From this land have come great cities, abundant farms, and majestic dreams.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:52.580
In 1871, this expansive nation sought to link its scattered population by building the world's

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.470
longest and costliest railroad.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:00.000
On November the 7th of 1885, it was completed.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.560
its thousands of miles of ribbon steel are told the story of a nation.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:11.680
Join me as we ride the Canadian, one of the last great trains of the world, on a journey

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:12.930
back in time.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.080
When the Canadian Parliament promised its people a railroad to the Pacific in only ten

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.020
years' time, it was done to coax the people of British Columbia into the newly formed

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.250
Confederation.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.460
B.C. agreed, and the builders started to work.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:48.380
Not surprisingly, it ran behind schedule and took fourteen, not ten years, to complete.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.180
Soon after the last spike was driven on the southern route of the Canadian Pacific, the

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.430
Grand Trunk started its own railroad to the north through the Yellowhead Pass.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.000
And simultaneously, the Canadian Northern, in competition, began

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.980
its own route and by the early part of the century Canada had not one but three

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:17.990
different transcontinental tracks. The early steam trains crossed the prairies

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:23.360
and mountains hauling settlers miners and people of the east. The trains were slow

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.140
and none too luxurious but they were a vast improvement over the Carlton Trail

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.000
that had moved pioneers

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:41.240
and adventurers before.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:48.310
And then on April the 24th of 1955,

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.170
70 years after the first trains rolled west,

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.300
the Canadian Pacific inaugurated one of the fastest

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.560
and most luxurious trains of the world,

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.000
the Canadia, a glittering new train of stainless steel

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.480
featuring two Observation Dome cars,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.560
one with a bullet-shaped lounge,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.420
the last car on the train,

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.940
and the Canadian's most distinctive feature.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.000
The train was an instant success.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.120
It was comfortable, fast,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.780
and passed through some of the most

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:18.440
beautiful scenery of the world.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.000
But within a few years, jets were making

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.440
the three-day trip to the west in only a few hours and the public started to

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.580
abandon the train. Via Rail, Canada's passenger rail service, operated the

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:44.920
Canadian on the southern route of the CP until 1989 when they put the Canadian on

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:51.010
the supercontinental's northerly route. At the same time, Via announced that they

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:56.530
were investing 200 million dollars in a program to completely restore this train.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:14.190
in montreal at the canadian national yards via is working to restore the rolling stock

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:20.440
of one of the world's great trains rather than to purchase new equipment it was decided to restore

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.720
the elegant stainless steel cars that were built for the canadian pacific by the bud company of

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:30.000
philadelphia in the early 1950s the cars

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.530
are brought into the yard and completely overhauled. The old steam heating systems are replaced

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:40.910
with new electric components that receive their power from the locomotive. The brakes,

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:46.410
plumbing, and lighting systems are replaced, and each car is meticulously restored down

00:05:46.410 --> 00:06:00.000
to new carpets, wallpaper, window shades, and linens. When the cars are finished, the

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.040
they are tested then put back together into a unit of usually 12 cars as a

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.500
newly restored train consisting of a baggage car, two coaches, the skyline dome,

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:16.020
a dining car, six sleepers, and the trademark park car with its dome and

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:22.440
bullet-shaped rear lounge. A total of 185 cars have been restored here at the

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.640
St. Charles Yards and the project is a testament to Canada and VIA's commitment

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.000
to restoring and saving the best

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:40.380
of the Railway Pass. The new route of the Canadian takes the northerly route of the

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.950
Old Supercontinental. From Toronto, the train heads around the Great Lakes to Winnipeg,

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:50.180
then west to Saskatoon and on to Edmonton. From Edmonton, the train climbs through the

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:55.560
Rockies, stopping in Jasper, and then proceeds on to Kamloops as it rolls towards Vancouver

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.000
on the Pacific coast. The Canadian begins its westward journey

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.060
Germany in the city of Toronto.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.050
Toronto is Canada's largest city.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.710
It is the capital of Ontario and one of the truly great cities of the world.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:18.830
It stands on Lake Ontario, a glittering metropolis of steel and eye-catching architecture.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:24.570
It has a population of over 3 million people and includes 80 different ethnic groups.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:29.370
The UN cited Toronto as the most ethnically diverse city in the world.

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:36.490
dominating the skyline is the CN Tower. It looms over the city and has become Toronto's

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:49.950
best-known landmark. Toronto is one of North America's most active cities. The sidewalks

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:54.430
are crowded with business people during the lunch hour, and at the end of the day, commuters

00:07:54.430 --> 00:08:00.000
use the city's extensive subway system, commuter rail services, and far-reaching public

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.380
transportation as they return to their homes. It is a city that has exploded in a few short

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:14.180
decades, from being a somewhat sleepy town to a dynamic, action-packed, world-class city.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:27.710
Canadian cities have done well in preserving their downtown cores. In most Canadian cities,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.000
vast indoor shopping malls have been

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.700
built in the center of the downtown. The Eaton Center on Yonge Street is one of

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.960
Canada's largest downtown shopping malls. It has helped to keep the central city a

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.520
vibrant center of retail trade by bringing to the people of Toronto a

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:51.250
style of shopping that has become expected. Canada's cold winters make

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:55.830
these enclosed cities within cities great places to shop.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:23.570
The old city hall built in 1899 stands next to the new city hall.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.840
Both are in the heart of downtown.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:29.730
Boating and sailing are popular activities during the summer months

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.980
ferries cross the water to the

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.820
many islands that stand just

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:36.870
offshore. Toronto was started

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.450
on the site of Fort York in

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:40.640
1793. Against

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.560
a backdrop of the city skyline,

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:44.700
a guide demonstrates how the muskets

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.820
were loaded and used against the Americans

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.800
during the War of 1812.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.240
When the war ended in 1815,

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:52.940
the long era of friendly

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:54.780
U.S. and Canadian relations

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.060
began. But we're here

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.000
to board the train. Downtown

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.900
only a short distance from the lake, stands Toronto's historic Union Station.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:12.260
An imposing stone structure built jointly by the Canadian Pacific and the Canadian National in 1927.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.400
Across the street is the Royal York Hotel.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.220
This is Canada's largest hotel with 1,600 rooms.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:25.800
It was built in 1929 by the Canadian Pacific as a natural extension of their rail services.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.000
Here at the Royal York, we'll spend the night before our departure.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:34.970
breakfast will be brought to us in our suite. The Canadian railroads built

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:39.650
towering castle-like hotels across the nation that are as much a part of the

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:44.830
railroad's history as the trains themselves. Shortly before our train is

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:48.370
scheduled to depart, we'll call for the bellman. He'll arrive at our door and

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:52.750
escort us and our luggage across the street into the station and take our bags

00:10:52.750 --> 00:11:00.000
directly to the train. Inside the cavernous main hall, the clock kicks off the minutes as the train

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:12.230
On the board, all of the scheduled departures are listed, including ours, the number one train to Vancouver, the Canadian, Canada's transcontinental train.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:15.800
The ticket agents work quickly, making changes.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:24.360
Unlike airline agents who arrange only seats, train agents handle the berths and must sort out sleeping car problems as well.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:30.000
The crowd gathers, awaiting the opening of the platform by the train personnel.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.680
Meanwhile, the train has arrived from the switching yard, and the VIA personnel are

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.080
busy trackside loading the train.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:44.770
Fuel must be loaded in both locomotives, pluggages brought to the baggage car, and the stewards

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:48.490
are busy tidying up the bedrooms and berths.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:53.590
The galley staff loads the food and supplies which will be needed on this transcontinental

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:57.810
trip to prepare meals for the passengers aboard the train.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:14.660
Once the preliminary preparations have been made, the station agent will open the platform

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:20.680
and the passengers will stroll alongside the silver train looking for their compartments and cars.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:33.950
At 12:45, the conductor shouts, "All aboard!"

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.950
The locomotives power up, the motors are engaged,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:40.490
and the Canadian pulls out of Toronto's Union Station

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:46.500
on a journey that will end three days and 2,800 miles later on the shores of BC.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:52.950
So sit back, relax, and welcome to the wonderful world of the train.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:13:00.000
From the observation car, passengers watch the city and suburbs

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.430
The suburbs roll by as the train makes its way north on its journey around the Great Lakes.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:21.550
As the day goes on, passengers gather in the park car and meet their fellow travelers.

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:27.490
At 5 o'clock, cocktails are made, hors d'oeuvres are served, and the routine of the train begins.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:46.360
While passengers are having their dinner on the first night of our ride,

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:49.860
the sleeping car porters are busy making up the berths.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:53.520
The Canadian has three types of sleeping accommodations.

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:57.550
Sections, which are sleeping berths in the front part of the car,

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:00.000
roomettes, where the single chair converts,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.450
into a bed and bedrooms, one of which you see here.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:07.120
Each bedroom has its own small bathroom,

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.530
two chairs and two berths.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:12.390
The steward makes up the beds by collapsing the chairs

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:14.550
and lowering the bunks into place.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.710
The space is not large, but the bedrooms are comfortable

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:19.640
and afford complete privacy.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:22.200
And devoted train travelers will tell you

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.240
there are a few things more relaxing

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.050
than sleeping aboard a train.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:35.000
The following morning, as the sun rises, we are passing over the Canadian Shield,

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:41.490
the 700-mile-wide slab of granite that separates the Great Lakes from Hudson Bay.

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:45.190
Newspapers are picked up in the stations along the way,

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:50.370
and passengers can relax in their berths and read the paper while the countryside rolls by.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:13.990
After enjoying breakfast in the dining car, passengers will spread out over the train.

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:19.610
Some return to their private rooms, others head to the Skyline Dome car to play cards.

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:25.810
Some talk or read, while others simply peer out of the window and watch the passing scenes.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:30.000
The weather is unpredictable. This morning rain rolls off the pan.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:37.200
panoramic windows of the observation dome. At 11 a.m., we pull into Sioux Lookout, a small town

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:43.260
above the Great Lakes. This is one of the service stops for the train. Here, passengers have about

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:49.300
20 minutes to get off and stretch their legs. The cars will be watered. Each car holds several

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:54.230
hundred gallons of water, and at designated stops, water will be added to the tanks.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:16:00.000
The train is fueled here by a young woman. Via Rail has been a lead

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.200
in employing women in non-traditional jobs.

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.460
With the train fueled and watered,

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:11.520
the conductor will call out all aboard,

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:13.720
the passengers hustle onto the train,

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:15.520
the stewards pull up the stairs,

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:16.740
lock the Dutch doors,

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:19.720
and the train is rolling again out over the shield

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:21.820
from Ontario into Manitoba

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:25.800
as we make our way west to the city of Winnipeg.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:43.120
The day passes quickly and the Canadian shield scenery of lakes and pines and granite walls turns into the prairies and then finally the skyline of Winnipeg.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:52.020
Winnipeg lies in the lower part of Manitoba.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:56.680
It is the provincial capital and home to 650,000 people.

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:00.000
It is sometimes called the Chicago of Canada.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:03.950
for it is the business centre of the Canadian prairies.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:09.340
The Canadian pulls into Union Station on Main Street in the heart of town.

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:11.450
Winnipeg has a modern skyline.

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:15.230
New office buildings tower over the prairie land.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:20.900
At Union Station, we leave the train and head across the street to the Fort Gary Hotel.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:27.270
The bellman follows with our bags, much as we experienced at the Royal York in Toronto.

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:30.000
The Fort Gary is one of Winnipeg's classic lands.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.270
It was built in the first part of the 20th century by the railroad and was designed in the famous chateau style.

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:45.270
Because of its location, across from the station, it was for years a center of activity for this crossroads town.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:52.360
Today its ballroom has been converted into a casino, the only place in Canada where casino gambling is allowed.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:18:00.000
Winnipeg is a bustling city that reflects the fact that this is a city of business.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:05.360
This community serves the business and financial needs of the vast prairie lands.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:11.290
Grain is shipped here, the prices determined on the Winnipeg Commodity Exchange, and the

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:16.850
banks and brokerage houses control much of the vast wealth the rich farmlands produce.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:22.390
The city is balmy and pleasant in the summer, but in winter it can be one of the chilliest

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:23.470
places of the world.

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:30.000
The corner of Main Street and Portage in Winnipeg is jokingly referred to as the coldest street-corner

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.930
on Earth. A complex system of enclosed walkways interconnects many of the

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:41.590
downtown buildings. Portage Place is a lavish multi-level shopping arcade

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:46.410
completely enclosed and designed to encourage people to shop and browse on

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:53.690
even the coldest days of the year. Winnipeg's best known store is The Bay, a

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:58.780
giant department store in the heart of town. Its name is actually the Hudson's

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:00.000
Bay Company.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:02.890
It was incorporated in 1670,

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:07.530
and the history of this store speaks of Winnipeg's early days.

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:10.490
To the north of Winnipeg lies Lower Fort Gary.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:14.500
This fort was built on the Red River by Hudson's Bay Company.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:16.800
The company established this outpost

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:19.100
to receive the furs caught by trappers

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:23.220
who braved this unsettled and largely unexplored land.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:26.530
The Red River carts were used to haul the furs.

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:29.830
They were also the primary means of moving goods west

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.000
the pioneers. They were to

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.140
Canada, but the covered wagons were

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:36.190
in the U.S. The carts

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:38.000
were made only of wood. When

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:39.880
the wheels turned, they created a

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:42.060
deafening squeak that was said to have driven

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:44.020
many settlers to distraction on

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:47.930
the long trips west. Winnipeg

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:49.670
was built on the Red River below the

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:51.290
fort at the point where the Red and

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:53.900
Assiniboine Rivers meet. Winnipeg

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:55.340
arose on the west bank,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.280
St. Boniface on the east.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:00.000
The historic St. Boniface Basilica

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.420
is one of many sites on this quiet and very French side of the river.

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:08.880
This is the largest French-speaking community west of Quebec.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.680
The Metais, French-Canadians who married natives,

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:16.600
settled here and began Winnipeg's long tradition of ethnic diversity.

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:23.650
The Ukrainian cathedral speaks of Winnipeg's second-largest ethnic group.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.490
Their heritage is evident throughout the city.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:30.000
There are more Ukrainians in Winnipeg than any other country.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.420
place in the world outside of the Ukraine.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:39.020
Winnipeg is the capital of Manitoba, and the legislative building stands in the heart of

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:39.440
town.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:43.450
It was built in 1919 in the neoclassical style.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:49.260
The golden boy atop the dome stands posed with a sheath of wheat in his hand, looking

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:51.480
out over the vast prairie lands.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.830
Winnipeg is home to the Royal Canadian Mint.

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:00.000
Here the coins of Canada are produced.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:05.170
The Centennial Concert Hall is the home of the Royal Winnipeg Ballet,

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:07.190
one of the world's foremost dance companies.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:10.080
The people of Winnipeg have always supported the arts.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:12.740
Winnipeg is covered with parks.

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:18.260
The green lawns and shade trees create a sense of restfulness in the summer months.

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:24.080
Assiniboine Park on the western edge of town is Winnipeg's oldest park.

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:30.000
It offers a variety of attractions, including a sculpture garden with the works of Leo Ma.

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:32.920
Leo Maul is a Winnipeg artist

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.760
whose work in bronze is recognized worldwide.

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:38.240
He has gained international attention

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.920
for his expertise at human detail.

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:46.380
On the Red River, excursion boats pass regularly.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.520
Winnipeg has always been a transportation town.

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:53.740
Its earliest days are, like so many Canadian towns,

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:57.280
tied directly to the fortunes of moving people and goods.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:00.000
Billowing clouds of smoke

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:04.740
moving across the prairies signaled the coming of the train.

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:09.140
The Prairie Dog Central is Winnipeg's last steam train.

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:16.830
It operates on an excursion journey today from Winnipeg's St. James Street Station out to Gross Isle.

00:22:16.830 --> 00:22:22.230
In the early 1900s, there were many of these trains that hauled people from Winnipeg

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:27.390
out to Victoria and Winnipeg Beach, Manitoba's lakefront towns.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:30.000
The locomotive was built in Scotland in

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:36.760
1882 and is typical of the hundreds of locomotives that travel the Canadian rails until the end of

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:51.390
the Age of Steam. Winnipeg Beach, north of the city, is one of many lake communities that provided

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.190
summer relief from the heat for the people of the town.

00:22:54.800 --> 00:23:00.000
It often comes as a surprise to visitors, but Manitoba is covered with enormous fresh

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.200
water lakes. The lakes are huge, a part of the Great Lakes system, left behind by melting

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:14.920
ice. Gimli, on the west side of Lake Winnipeg, is a commercial fishing port. Gimli is a part

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:21.340
of Manitoba's varied ethnic heritage. This community is Icelandic, and boasts the largest

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:28.060
population of Icelanders outside of Iceland itself. It is also the Purple Martin capital

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:30.000
of Manitoba.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:43.550
purple Martin houses stand near the harbor at the water's edge. But Winnipeg is

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:49.490
mostly about business. This is a town of trade. New high-rise office buildings

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:54.790
cover the downtown streets. Banks provide employment and financing and keep the

00:23:54.790 --> 00:24:00.000
wheels of commerce turning from the Great Lakes West. Winnipeg's comparison

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.700
to Chicago is because of this, the Winnipeg Commodity Exchange.

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:10.650
Like the Chicago Board of Trade, this exchange room creates and controls the fortunes of

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:13.120
the grain-producing region.

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:18.420
Here the prices are set for the bushels and boxcars of everything, from oats standing

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:23.660
in a barn to crops that have not only not been harvested yet, they haven't even been

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:25.210
planted yet.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:30.000
The prices are set, haggled over, and bid up and down on the floor of this exchange

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:37.710
Exchange. Weather is vital, and charts showing rainfall in specific regions, and forecasts for

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:45.730
the months ahead are used by the traders in making their bids. At first glance, these trading floors

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:51.490
always seem chaotic with no point of order. But there is, of course, a highly defined system of

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:56.910
doing things here, and at the end of the trading day, all of the paperwork is processed, and farmers,

00:24:56.910 --> 00:24:59.670
bankers, and investors across Canada

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.700
seen the value of their assets

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:04.770
rise or fall. This exchange

00:25:04.770 --> 00:25:06.530
sets the heartbeat of the prairie's

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.650
economic health. From Winnipeg

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:11.940
we now head west. The Canadian

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:13.800
leaves Union Station through

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:15.700
the Winnipeg Yards and heads

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:17.780
out over the prairies on a track

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:19.860
as straight as an arrow to our next

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:21.640
stop at Saskatoon.

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:42.130
Operating the train is a highly skilled job.

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:47.250
Like piloting a plane or guiding a ship, the engineer is ultimately responsible for the

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:51.000
lives of hundreds of people on board the train.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:55.960
The locomotive is staffed by two people, an engineer and his assistant.

00:25:55.960 --> 00:25:59.480
In reality, they both take turns driving the train.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:02.950
The Canadian is pulled by two locomotives,

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:05.780
though only the head engine is staffed.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:08.400
The engineer keeps his hands busy between the throttle,

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:11.040
which has four positions, and the air brakes,

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.810
which are the train's most critical component.

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:17.590
Various gauges are monitored as the engineer keeps an eye

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:19.440
on the machinery behind.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:21.320
The horn button announces the train

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.720
to the road crossings ahead.

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:27.460
The most closely watched instrument is the speedometer.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:30.000
The engineer constantly monitors the speed

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.530
to the train, particularly as the signals and the track switches are approached.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:41.570
These giant locomotives are diesel electric, F-40s manufactured by General Electric in

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:42.650
Hamilton, Ontario.

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:48.960
Each produces 1,800 horsepower, and they operate by burning diesel fuel in generators

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:54.480
that make electricity, which is used to power the traction motors that actually move the

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:54.820
train.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:27:00.000
The system is efficient, dependable, and creates a smooth and more even rod.

00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:14.630
Our next stop is Saskatoon, Saskatchewan's largest city, located near the center of the province, northwest of Regina the capital, 150 miles away.

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:29.440
The train pulls in at dawn. The schedule calls for an arrival at 3 a.m., but a forest fire in Ontario has delayed this westbound train, and as a result, we are running several hours behind.

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:36.540
saskatoon is a surprise a bustling prairie town built on the south saskatchewan river

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:44.940
a city of bluffs bridges and wide tree-lined streets the population is 185 000 and the

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:50.860
community serves the retail medical and business needs of farmers and their families

00:27:50.860 --> 00:27:59.800
scattered over much of the province saskatoon's best known site is a hotel

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:06.050
The Castle Lake Vesborough Hotel was built on the bluffs of the river in 1935 by the Canadian National,

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:09.210
another of the great Railway Chateau hotels.

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:13.770
But this community is defined more by what surrounds the town.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:20.990
Rich, abundant farms, acres and acres of prairie that in late summer glisten with golden wheat.

00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:30.000
Saskatchewan is a part of the rich plains of North America that begin in Texas and Oklahoma and reach north of Saskatoon.

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.950
Livestock is raised on pasture lands

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:34.850
and the whole region is not much

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:36.890
different from what you would find outside

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:39.990
a Kansas town. The grain

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.970
elevators of Canada stand like

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.630
sentinels across the prairies.

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:45.810
They hug the tracks and box

00:28:45.810 --> 00:28:47.730
cars of grain move from here to the

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:49.290
four corners of the world.

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:52.040
Canada's grain elevators are

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:54.180
almost always of metal, square

00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:56.020
with pitched roofs. Very

00:28:56.020 --> 00:28:58.090
different from the round concrete

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:00.000
prairie skyscrapers found

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:00.900
in the U.S.

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:05.570
Saskatchewan was Indian land.

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:10.630
Long before the European settlers arrived, nomadic Indians roamed these plains.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:16.900
Along the banks of the South Saskatchewan River, tribes would stay for extended periods

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:17.520
of time.

00:29:18.310 --> 00:29:20.810
The Indians followed the buffalo herds.

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:26.600
This was an ideal spot because of the river bluffs where the buffalo could be driven over

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.960
the edge to their deaths below.

00:29:31.790 --> 00:29:36.030
Today at this spot, the provincial government has created a heritage park,

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:44.110
Wanaskawin. It means seeking peace of mind. It is an interpretive Indian site where you can

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:49.430
explore the land and wander through exhibits that tell the stories of the lives of these

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:55.580
nomadic people. Their lives depended on the buffalo. These animals provided the Indians

00:29:55.580 --> 00:30:00.000
with everything they would need. At Wanaskawin today, native

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:05.690
North Americans demonstrate dances, storytelling, and the use of beads. The

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:11.100
beads were used for artistic expression as well as currency on the plains. They

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:16.120
were woven into intricate designs and were highly prized, traded, and given as

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:24.700
gifts. Today at Wanuskewin, the University of Saskatchewan operates an

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:30.000
extensive archaeological laboratory. Students of archaeology learn first

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:35.510
hand the patient skills a modern archaeologist needs. These were nomadic

00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:40.690
people, so nothing was moved unless it was needed. They left behind a rich

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:45.830
treasure trove of pots, clothing fragments, and what was essentially their

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:51.850
trash. Today these discarded fragments of an early civilization yield answers

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:56.130
about these people's lives and the movements and advancements of people

00:30:56.130 --> 00:31:00.000
over the North American plains. The nomadic people

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.500
of North America left behind much to be discovered, and a great deal of modern

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:09.580
archaeology involves uncovering, examining, and studying the movements of

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:14.460
these tribes as we seek to understand how the North American continent developed

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:23.020
and evolved. From Saskatoon, the train continues west. Our train tonight is

00:31:23.020 --> 00:31:30.000
running on time, and we will depart Saskatoon at 3:38 a.m. Not a convenient time, but the reality

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:34.710
of cross-country trains is that some of the stops will inevitably be in the

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:39.390
middle of the night. The awaiting passengers board and the train heads out

00:31:39.390 --> 00:31:44.920
into the black empty skies. The stewards are busy preparing the berths for

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:49.770
passengers who have booked sleeping accommodations out of Saskatoon. This is

00:31:49.770 --> 00:31:54.250
a section. These are berths at the front of the sleeping car that are made out of

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:58.950
daytime seating areas. The steward will lower the beds and make up the berths

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:00.000
that line the corridor.

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:00.980
corridor.

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:08.040
To ensure privacy, he'll hang heavy canvas curtains on both the upper and the lower berths.

00:32:08.040 --> 00:32:12.500
Changing into your pajamas behind these curtains has challenged travelers since the first sleeping

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:16.680
car started to roll.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:21.280
The night passes and the following morning, as the sun rises over the prairies, we're

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:27.620
heading west, leaving Saskatchewan and entering the eastern plains of Alberta.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:41.080
breakfast is a great time on board the train the smell of the bacon the rocking

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:45.560
of the dining car and the chattering of the rails make for an unusual and

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:50.550
interesting way to start your day in the galley the chef will flip pancakes on

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:55.360
the griddle using balancing skills that are unique to a train the train is

00:32:55.360 --> 00:33:00.000
filled with travelers from around the world young and old couples family

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.560
and single people all meet on board

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:05.610
the train. The space is small and you

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:07.130
can't help but meet and make new

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:09.730
friends. Families with children are

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:11.900
always a welcome sight. What could be

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:14.120
more magical for a child than a ride on

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:17.630
a train? As the train heads west the

00:33:17.630 --> 00:33:20.800
morning activities begin. Stewards roam

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:22.900
the corridors looking for rooms where

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:25.580
the passengers are temporarily gone so

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:28.150
they can stow away the birds. In the

00:33:28.150 --> 00:33:30.000
morning the beds are stripped and tucked

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.960
away. The chairs put back out and the bedroom once again becomes a private

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:39.980
sitting room. This procedure of changing from a sitting to a sleeping room goes

00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:47.140
on twice a day every day the train is rolling along. Sometimes the public areas

00:33:47.140 --> 00:34:00.000
are quiet and empty and passengers will have the space all to themselves. The

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:06.100
important person on the train. Passengers sometimes assume the engineer driving the train

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:12.830
is the number one person, but not so. The engineer and everyone else on board answers ultimately

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:19.210
to the conductor, who is the true captain of the train. The conductor has many jobs. He takes

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:25.870
tickets, but handling the paperwork is the more time-consuming task. He must also monitor the

00:34:25.870 --> 00:34:30.000
timetable, hustled the staff to move the train out of the station quickly

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:32.620
when it's running behind, handle staff problems,

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.880
passenger complaints, and in general,

00:34:35.180 --> 00:34:36.660
keep the train rolling along.

00:34:37.020 --> 00:34:39.800
He is the most influential person on board,

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:43.460
and he has the ultimate say on the operation of the train.

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.910
From Saskatoon, we head west into Alberta

00:34:47.910 --> 00:34:51.310
and out over the eastern plains of this very province.

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:55.880
The terrain starts to rise as we approach our next stop,

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:58.130
the city of Edmonton.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:01.740
Edmonton is Alberta's capital.

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:03.810
It lies north of Alberta's

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:05.310
other large city, Calgary.

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:07.230
Both are oil towns.

00:35:07.630 --> 00:35:09.390
Edmonton is the older of the two,

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:11.610
started as a fort before the railroad

00:35:11.610 --> 00:35:14.010
arrived. The Canadian

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:16.130
pulls into the station right on schedule

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:17.730
at 8.35 a.m.

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:20.180
Edmonton stands on the

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:21.620
North Saskatchewan River.

00:35:22.150 --> 00:35:24.070
Its skyline is new, glittering

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:25.940
and tall. The town reflects

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:27.990
the economic impact Alberta

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:30.000
oil has had on Canada's

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:37.730
This is the provincial capital, and the legislature building in the center of town is impressive and bold.

00:35:39.290 --> 00:35:43.850
Edmonton's strategic location earned it the title of Gateway to the North.

00:35:44.490 --> 00:35:49.630
Thousands of tons of food, medical supplies, and mail passed through Edmonton

00:35:49.630 --> 00:35:53.250
on its way to Canada's northwest in the early days.

00:35:53.250 --> 00:36:00.000
The Grand Trunk Railway built the imposing McDonaldhood,

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:06.610
hotel on a cliff overlooking the river. Today, this property has been restored much like the

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:17.200
Canadian itself. In front of the hotel on Saturday nights, a bagpiper welcomes guests who are staying

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:23.940
and dining in this grand old hotel. The lobby is hushed and dramatic. Through these corridors

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:29.100
have passed thousands of men and women of Edmonton's social and business world.

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:36.180
On Sundays, the Mac, as it is affectionately known,

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:39.520
welcomes Edmonton people with a lavish Sunday brunch

00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:42.000
served in the grand ballroom of the hotel.

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:47.560
Brunch at the Mac has been popular in Edmonton since the hotel was restored.

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:50.450
The setting is one of the most elegant to be found

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:54.010
between the Great Lakes to the east and the Rocky Mountains to the west.

00:36:54.010 --> 00:37:00.000
Ten minutes from downtown in Edmonton's picturesque River Valley,

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:01.560
is Fort Edmonton Park,

00:37:01.880 --> 00:37:03.940
Canada's largest historical park.

00:37:04.730 --> 00:37:06.170
This is a historic settlement

00:37:06.170 --> 00:37:08.170
where each street represents

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.590
a different decade of Edmonton life.

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:13.600
Four different areas tell of Edmonton

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:15.380
when it was a fur trading post,

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:16.800
a gold rush town,

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:18.960
a turn-of-the-century ragtime city,

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:20.920
and a roaring 20s town.

00:37:21.740 --> 00:37:23.480
Several attractions emphasize

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:25.300
the role of transportation.

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:28.490
Stagecoaches, wagons, and trolleys

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:30.000
are a part of this historic city.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:39.320
Here you can ride an old-fashioned streetcar just like the type that once ran down Jasper Avenue, Edmonton's main street.

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:52.240
A steam train rumbles along another of the giant locomotives that originally conquered the Canadian West.

00:38:04.760 --> 00:38:09.720
The cab is manned by an engineer and a fireman, just like the modern-day Canadian.

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:14.060
Except on these old trains, the fireman's job was just that.

00:38:14.470 --> 00:38:20.590
He stoked the boiler and kept the fires hot so the engine would make steam and keep the train rolling along.

00:38:21.820 --> 00:38:25.220
But Edmonton's biggest attraction is the West Edmonton Mall.

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:30.000
When you see the replica of a Spanish galleon, you'll know this is no ordinary showroom.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:30.680
shopping mall.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:32.100
This is the largest

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:32.880
shopping mall

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:33.660
in the world.

00:38:34.530 --> 00:38:35.370
It cost over

00:38:35.370 --> 00:38:36.590
one billion dollars

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:37.170
to build.

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:37.930
It covers

00:38:37.930 --> 00:38:39.570
five million square feet,

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:40.470
the equivalent

00:38:40.470 --> 00:38:42.890
of 115 football fields.

00:38:43.310 --> 00:38:43.910
It includes

00:38:43.910 --> 00:38:44.930
800 stores,

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:46.810
110 restaurants,

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:48.410
19 movie theaters,

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:49.390
a zoo,

00:38:49.670 --> 00:38:50.610
an amusement park.

00:38:50.610 --> 00:38:51.630
The whole thing

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:52.370
is staggering

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:53.390
in its size.

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:55.410
There is a dolphin lagoon

00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:56.550
with four Atlantic

00:38:56.550 --> 00:38:57.830
bottlenose dolphins,

00:38:58.540 --> 00:39:00.000
a giant skating ring

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:04.680
open year-round, and enough attractions to keep you busy for a month.

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:09.880
The World Water Park includes the world's largest indoor swimming pool,

00:39:10.180 --> 00:39:14.120
complete with a wade-making machine and huge water slides.

00:39:14.660 --> 00:39:18.760
It's a giant, enclosed Coney Island in the Canadian North.

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:34.740
And what would a shopping mall be without bungee jumping?

00:39:34.740 --> 00:39:38.220
Yes, you can buy a car or a pair of sneakers

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:41.320
and jump off of a monstrously high perch

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:42.740
and bounce up and down.

00:39:42.740 --> 00:40:00.000
And if that isn't enough, there's a full-scale amusement

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:06.420
park with merry-go-rounds ferris wheels and 22 other attractions and rides including a roller

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:13.240
coaster that will spin you upside down the west edmonton mall is unique it's edmonton's

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:17.000
best known attraction and unlike anything else in the world

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:41.630
edmonton is a great city sophisticated cultured fun and canada's gateway to the north the following

00:40:41.630 --> 00:40:50.240
morning at 905 the canadian departs from edmonton to the west we have a new head in crew on board

00:40:50.240 --> 00:41:00.000
that will take us on to the rockies today the scenery changes low plains give way to rising terrain as the mountains

00:41:00.120 --> 00:41:10.010
loom up ahead. We pass endless freights loaded with grain moving west to the

00:41:10.010 --> 00:41:16.890
Canadian ports. The train builds speed as we head west past lakes and valleys onto

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:30.000
the Rocky Mountain range. The passengers crowd in the observation dome to view the scenery which

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:48.800
is the main attraction of the day. The train curves back and forth around the lakes and

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:54.750
mountains as the land rises rapidly at the foot of the range. We are heading west through

00:41:54.750 --> 00:42:00.000
the Rockies towards the Yellowhead Pass. When the original surveys of the mountains

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:06.690
were done in the 1870s, there was endless debate about which pass to use. The Canadian Pacific

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:13.330
opted for the Kicking Horse Pass to the south. It wasn't until 1915 when the Grand Trunk and

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:19.870
Canadian Northern began building their lines west that the Yellowhead Pass was used. We will be

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:25.730
passing through the Yellowhead Pass as we move toward the Pacific coast. But before we do, we're

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:30.000
We're going to leave the train at Jasper, Alberta for a few days of adventure

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.260
in the heart of the Canadian Rockies.

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:35.000
The Canadian approaches Jasper

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:37.100
on the mainline tracks of the Canadian

00:42:37.100 --> 00:42:39.100
National. This was the route

00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:40.560
of the supercontinental,

00:42:40.940 --> 00:42:42.840
CN's transcontinental train.

00:42:43.890 --> 00:42:45.470
Jasper stands in the center

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:47.490
of Jasper National Park.

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:49.590
Unlike Banff to the south,

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:51.570
which is mostly a tourist town,

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:53.650
Jasper was, for many years,

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:55.190
an active commercial center.

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:57.820
The Canadian National maintained

00:42:57.820 --> 00:43:00.000
large railroad yards here, and

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.320
the town was a primary switching point as the freights were assembled to head east and

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:13.270
west. Jasper feels like a chalet village. The architecture is vaguely Bavarian. In summer,

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:18.270
it's very busy with travelers from around the world. Some arrive by train, others come

00:43:18.270 --> 00:43:25.430
in on coaches and by car. This is mountain country. Surrounding Jasper are some of the

00:43:25.430 --> 00:43:30.000
most spectacular mountains of the world. This is toward the northern end of

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:31.760
the massive Rocky Mountain Range

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.360
that begins in New Mexico

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:35.500
and ends north of British Columbia.

00:43:36.140 --> 00:43:37.990
The mountains are somewhat lower

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:39.390
than those in the U.S.

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:42.810
Most of the peaks are between 9,000 and 11,000 feet.

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:44.660
But the shapes are very dramatic.

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:47.760
The spring runoff creates churning rivers

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:49.160
that drop dramatically,

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:52.220
creating some of the world's most impressive waterfalls.

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:55.190
Outside of the town of Jasper

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:56.810
stands the Jasper Park Lodge.

00:43:58.100 --> 00:44:00.000
The Jasper Park Lodge had very hot,

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:01.410
humble beginnings.

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:04.710
It started out as a tent camp in 1915,

00:44:04.710 --> 00:44:07.150
begun by Fred and Jack Brewster,

00:44:07.150 --> 00:44:10.600
who were the tourism pioneers of this region.

00:44:10.600 --> 00:44:14.840
In 1921, the Grand Trunk Railroad took over the tent camp

00:44:14.840 --> 00:44:17.500
and built eight log cabins.

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:19.920
The setting is beautiful on Lake Beauvais,

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:22.700
with Mount Edith Cavell looming ahead.

00:44:22.700 --> 00:44:28.060
Over the years, it has expanded into a 437-room resort.

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:30.000
One of its most interesting features

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.430
is its highly mobile room service.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:36.450
If you call room service here and order breakfast, lunch, or dinner,

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:45.720
it will be delivered by a bellman on a bicycle, regardless of the weather.

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:50.800
The Lodge is a true resort offering endless outdoor activities.

00:44:50.800 --> 00:44:54.510
You can rent a canoe or a kayak and paddle across the lake,

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:59.250
and tennis courts offer magnificent vistas into the Rocky Mountain Range.

00:45:05.180 --> 00:45:07.060
The golf course is world-renowned.

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:11.980
Built in 1924, it took 200 men and 50 teams of horses to create.

00:45:12.450 --> 00:45:16.670
It was designed by the world-famous golf architect Stanley Thompson.

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:23.670
Horseback riding has been a popular activity here

00:45:23.670 --> 00:45:26.290
since the Brewsters pitched their first tents

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:28.990
and started inviting travelers from the East.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:35.520
day, the trail masters saddle up the horses and lead people staying at the lodge on the trails

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:42.960
around the lake, past rivers, and through valleys of the mountain rain. Magnificent scenery forms

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:49.380
a backdrop along the trails, and the experience is typical of the outdoor adventures this region

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:50.040
provides.

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:10.340
Through the region, pass several churning rivers that flow out of the mountains and lakes.

00:46:11.030 --> 00:46:15.410
Whitewater rafting is one of the activities the Jasper Park Lodge provides.

00:46:16.350 --> 00:46:21.750
Participants carry the rafts to the river's edge and receive a few instructions before starting out.

00:46:21.750 --> 00:46:30.000
And depending on your mood and sense of daring, you can experience anything from a gentle float trip to a hair-raising rapids adventure.

00:46:30.800 --> 00:46:34.330
Today we're going to run the Athabascan River.

00:46:34.330 --> 00:46:40.250
This journey starts below the Athabascan Falls, and we'll run the river for about 10 miles.

00:46:40.250 --> 00:46:45.050
The whole thing takes about 45 minutes, and it's a pretty good way to, quite literally,

00:46:45.050 --> 00:47:00.000
get your feet wet.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.310
Like a bigger thrill, the lodge will take you the other direction, to the Moline River.

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:08.230
This is one of the Rockies' most beautiful spots.

00:47:08.230 --> 00:47:13.050
The river joins Moline and Medicine Lakes, and when they hand you a helmet, you'll know

00:47:13.050 --> 00:47:16.520
this ride is going to be a little less calm.

00:47:16.520 --> 00:47:19.010
This is a true whitewater trip.

00:47:19.010 --> 00:47:24.020
The river is running at nearly full force, and the raft runners will guarantee you a

00:47:24.020 --> 00:47:25.160
dramatic ride.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:36.210
The raft set out from Moline Lake and enter the outflow as they start this wild river journey.

00:48:12.480 --> 00:48:24.620
Hifting in the Rockies is one of the highlights of staying at the Jasper Park Lodge.

00:48:24.620 --> 00:48:29.540
For other travelers, the Jasper Tramway offers its own thrills.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.420
This is the longest cable car in Canada.

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:39.200
It opened in 1964 and takes 30 passengers at a time to the top of Whistler Mountain.

00:48:39.910 --> 00:48:45.010
The ride takes seven minutes and crosses one tower en route to the mountain peak.

00:48:45.010 --> 00:48:54.550
A conductor on board offers a running commentary as people peer out of the car into the Rocky Mountain Range.

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:07.240
At the summit, passengers leave the tram and explore the mountaintop.

00:49:07.240 --> 00:49:12.930
The giant wheels turn rapidly as the cable car makes its way back down the slope.

00:49:14.060 --> 00:49:15.200
This is Whistler Mountain.

00:49:15.730 --> 00:49:21.150
It is about 600 million years old and consists mainly of sandstone and shale.

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:25.990
It receives its name from the whistling marmots that cover the slopes.

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:30.000
The temperature drops rapidly, and though it was almost...

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.800
almost hot at the base, a jacket and sweater are a must-up here. Even in June, when this

00:49:36.800 --> 00:49:44.490
film was shot, some snow remains. From the top, you can see 70 miles west to Mount Robeson

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:50.660
and 60 miles south to the Columbia Icefields. It's one of the best distance-viewing spots

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:52.300
in the entire mountain range.

00:49:54.180 --> 00:50:00.000
Connecting Jasper and Lake Louise is the Icefields Parkway. It is no exaggeration,

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:07.010
to say that this is one of the most beautiful stretches of road in the world. For 140 miles,

00:50:07.010 --> 00:50:18.620
the view is truly mesmerizing. One breathtaking mountain after another.

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:24.100
The Icefields Parkway passes through the center of Canada's two best known national parks,

00:50:24.100 --> 00:50:30.000
Jasper and Banff. Jasper National Park includes the town of Jasper and stretches

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:32.000
south to the Columbia Ice Field.

00:50:32.740 --> 00:50:38.160
The park offers outdoor activities ranging from bicycling to camping and hiking through

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:38.980
the range.

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:45.400
The scenery is inspiring, the scale almost beyond words.

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:30.000
For many visitors, Jasper National Park's greatest thrill is its abundance.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:36.500
abundant wildlife. Few places of the world offer such a collection of wild animals roaming

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:41.920
everywhere you go. Mountain goats are so common, cars often have trouble getting through on

00:51:41.920 --> 00:51:42.840
the roads.

00:52:09.950 --> 00:52:13.490
Deer and elk will graze right down to the edge of the camps.

00:52:13.990 --> 00:52:17.750
And the bighorn sheep are so tame that you can get remarkably close.

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:22.390
Jasper National Park is a wildlife photographer's dream.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:32.870
Even the bears seem unaffected by people.

00:52:32.870 --> 00:52:39.720
These scenes were taken right alongside the Icefields Parkway with the cars buzzing by.

00:52:39.720 --> 00:52:53.630
Wildlife abounds in this region of beautiful lakes and snow-capped peaks,

00:52:53.630 --> 00:53:00.000
but the region's most popular attractions are the massive glaciers that spill out of the Columbia Ice

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:07.050
fields and down the mountain sides. The Brewster brothers, who hold almost legendary status

00:53:07.050 --> 00:53:13.660
in these parts, built this chalet to house visitors to the Athabascan Glacier. The Brewsters

00:53:13.660 --> 00:53:19.920
developed a bus on big tires they called a snow coach. The first ones were fairly primitive,

00:53:20.320 --> 00:53:25.780
but over the years they have grown into these Star War-like vehicles that move people over

00:53:25.780 --> 00:53:30.000
the glacier for an adventure on the ice. This is a

00:53:30.030 --> 00:53:35.490
bumpy ride the coach is moving along at about four miles an hour and in spite of

00:53:35.490 --> 00:53:41.340
the tire size the rocking is pretty severe each spring a new snow trail has

00:53:41.340 --> 00:53:47.150
to be created this is not a permanent road it is always melting freezing

00:53:47.150 --> 00:53:53.430
snowing and melting again this is not a stable trail though the ice is actually

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:55.060
several hundred feet thick

00:54:03.580 --> 00:54:08.770
At the end of the snowy road, the passengers disembark and walk out onto the ice.

00:54:08.770 --> 00:54:13.410
This was the first Glacier Walk attraction up North America.

00:54:13.410 --> 00:54:20.620
Since it began, thousands of people have had the experience of wandering out over the Athabascan Glacier

00:54:20.620 --> 00:54:25.320
that spills out of the massive Columbia Ice Field high overhead.

00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:49.260
From the Athabascan Glacier, we continue south and soon pass from Jasper into Banff National Park.

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:59.140
The Icefields Parkway connects the Columbia Icefields with Lake Louise and Banff to the south.

00:55:06.520 --> 00:55:16.460
At times the scale of the scenery, the size of the mountains, the height of the waterfalls and the thickness of the ice cause us to overlook nature's more delicate sights.

00:55:16.460 --> 00:55:22.880
Lovely wildflowers bloom from spring to autumn, covering the fields and valley floors.

00:55:36.810 --> 00:55:43.410
At the southern end of the parkway, we arrive at two of Canada's best-known destinations, Lake Louise and Banff.

00:55:43.410 --> 00:55:55.230
The tracks of the Canadian Pacific pass through these resorts, and for 34 years, the Canadian took the southern route, stopping in Banff and Lake Louise rather than Jasper to the north.

00:55:56.460 --> 00:56:00.000
Lake Louise is one of the most picturesque spots on Earth.

00:56:00.830 --> 00:56:03.490
It stands in front of towering mountains,

00:56:03.490 --> 00:56:05.940
glaciers pouring off the slopes.

00:56:05.940 --> 00:56:26.340
The spot is almost mystical at times.

00:56:26.340 --> 00:56:30.000
On the shores of the lake stand the world-famous Chateau Lake

00:56:30.060 --> 00:56:36.510
like the Jasper Park Lodge its origins were humble initially a small cabin for

00:56:36.510 --> 00:56:41.300
passengers off of the train but the railroad saw the potential of the spot

00:56:41.300 --> 00:56:48.000
and erected a hotel on the site worthy as a travel destination unto itself giant

00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:57.480
yellow poppies surround the hotel and grow wild in the nearby fields the

00:56:57.480 --> 00:57:00.000
Canadian Pacific Railroad built and continued

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:01.740
to operate this hotel.

00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:08.430
At one end of the lobby is one of the most glorious picture windows of the world, framing

00:57:08.430 --> 00:57:10.430
a true postcard view.

00:57:17.160 --> 00:57:20.580
Feeling inside is that of a Bavarian chalet.

00:57:21.350 --> 00:57:28.360
The atmosphere is old world, friendly and warm, a carryback to another time, when travel

00:57:28.360 --> 00:57:30.000
was less rushed and pastored.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:50.390
people traveling through Canada on the Canadian could stop and spend a few days.

00:57:50.390 --> 00:57:53.510
Nearby to Lake Louise is Moraine Lake.

00:57:53.510 --> 00:57:57.500
This is often called the most photographed lake in the world.

00:57:57.500 --> 00:58:00.000
Millions of cameras have been aimed at this spot.

00:58:01.150 --> 00:58:05.470
This vista is even printed on the back of the Canadian $20 bill.

00:58:15.170 --> 00:58:18.310
On to the east, a short distance, is the town of Banff.

00:58:18.310 --> 00:58:25.390
This is a charming, though somewhat commercialized, spot that still does retain some of its turn-of-the-century character.

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:29.990
Banff is best known for the Banff Springs Association.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:35.020
This colossal pile, and there is no other word that describes it so well,

00:58:35.020 --> 00:58:40.020
was built in 1888 as the first of the great railway hotels.

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:43.130
A statue of William Cornelius Van Horn,

00:58:43.130 --> 00:58:48.130
chairman of the Canadian Pacific when the railroad was built, stands out front.

00:58:48.130 --> 00:58:51.210
A plaque is inscribed with his best-known quote,

00:58:51.210 --> 00:58:56.010
"Since we can't export the scenery, we'll have to import the tourists."

00:58:56.010 --> 00:59:00.000
Inside the hotel is an image

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:06.920
collection of old silver plate off the Canadian Pacific's early trains. Much of

00:59:06.920 --> 00:59:12.480
this heavy railroad silver was used aboard the original Canadian from 1955

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:14.720
until 1989.

00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:39.280
One of BAMP's best-known attractions is the gondola lift that runs to the top of Sulphur Mountain.

00:59:40.150 --> 00:59:43.970
The ride offers a panoramic view along the way.

01:00:01.150 --> 01:00:08.130
At the summit, your view stretches as far as the eye can see as you take in the scope and size of the Rocky Mountain Rain.

01:00:15.100 --> 01:00:20.900
From Banff, we now drive back to Jasper, turn in our rental car, and prepare to re-board the train.

01:00:21.430 --> 01:00:28.670
At Jasper, the train is serviced, fuel is added, the cars are watered, the brakes checked, and a new engine crew is brought on board.

01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:29.960
Here are the windows.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:35.600
are voiced. Two thousand miles of travel has left more than a few dead bugs on the front part of the

01:00:35.600 --> 01:00:42.290
observation dome. From Jasper, the train now heads west toward the Yellowhead Pass.

01:01:00.320 --> 01:01:05.440
today the canadian rolls on the tracks of the canadian national through the northern yellowhead

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:12.990
pass but this low easy pass was not the first one used when the canadian pacific began to build

01:01:12.990 --> 01:01:18.670
canada's first transcontinental train nothing caused more argument than the selection of the

01:01:18.670 --> 01:01:23.840
pass the yellowhead was easy it was low and the grades could be contained

01:01:23.840 --> 01:01:30.000
the kicking horse pass to the south was high and would require grades that were dangerously

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:36.620
steep. Today as the Canadian heads west through the Yellowhead Pass, we travel alongside

01:01:36.620 --> 01:01:44.770
spectacular scenery. Mount Robeson, Canada's highest peak, looms off to the north. Why

01:01:44.770 --> 01:01:51.750
wasn't this easy pass used? Why the difficult southern pass? It was because of the Americans.

01:01:52.640 --> 01:02:00.000
At the time, Canada's west was almost completely unsettled. There was fear that the Americans would simply walk across

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:03.830
cross the 49th parallel and declare the land their own.

01:02:03.830 --> 01:02:08.620
So the southern route, closer to the border with the difficult and dangerous Kicking Horse

01:02:08.620 --> 01:02:15.140
Pass was chosen so the area would be settled quickly in order to keep the Americans away.

01:02:32.720 --> 01:02:35.600
Dinner in the diner, nothing could be finer.

01:02:35.600 --> 01:02:40.690
An old refrain from a nostalgic song, and today it's as true as ever.

01:02:40.690 --> 01:02:47.360
Dining aboard the Canadian is one of the most relaxing aspects of this travel experience.

01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:53.680
Passengers order from a fairly extensive menu, and everything is prepared to order on board.

01:02:53.680 --> 01:02:59.480
There are no microwaves in the dining car, all of the food is prepared fresh each day.

01:03:00.250 --> 01:03:05.100
working in the galley is a job that requires a good sense of balance the

01:03:05.100 --> 01:03:10.640
Canadian runs on continuous welded track and the ride is fairly smooth but the

01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:15.580
80 to 90 mile an hour speed makes for enough jostling in the dining car and

01:03:15.580 --> 01:03:23.100
galley to keep the chefs and dining car stewards on their toes dining cars on

01:03:23.100 --> 01:03:30.000
board trains are a great place to make new friends everyone must share tables otherwise all

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:31.840
of the passengers couldn't be served.

01:03:32.500 --> 01:03:37.820
And after introductions are made, most passengers find their nightly table mates to be interesting.

01:03:38.560 --> 01:03:43.160
Everyone shares, by virtue of the trip, a common love of trains.

01:04:01.400 --> 01:04:14.880
As we finish our dinner, the Canadian is heading west, coming out of the Rockies and heading out over the rolling highlands of central BC en route to our next stop, the historic mining town of Kamloops.

01:04:18.810 --> 01:04:24.370
Kamloops is, if nothing else, a railroad town.

01:04:24.370 --> 01:04:29.570
The main lines of the Canadian National and Canadian Pacific meet at this point.

01:04:30.120 --> 01:04:38.010
Kamloops lies in the lower central part of British Columbia, between the Rockies to the east, and the Coast Mountains to the west.

01:04:38.010 --> 01:04:45.680
The population is about 70,000, and the feeling is that of a rugged railroad and ranching town.

01:04:45.680 --> 01:04:53.420
The city hugs the Thompson River that runs through the heart of town, and the rail lines and extensive switching yards

01:04:53.420 --> 01:05:00.000
make the trains an integral part of the commerce and economy of the region. A small excursion

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:05.780
boat takes people down the river and lets them see how quickly the city disappears and

01:05:05.780 --> 01:05:23.170
the ranch lands begin. This is farm country, but not quite the same as the prairie lands.

01:05:23.170 --> 01:05:30.000
Here there are rolling hills. The land is much more picturesque, and the farms tend to be somewhat smaller in

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:31.000
their operations.

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:37.970
There are extensive dairy operations in this part of BC, and the scenes of cattle, horses,

01:05:37.970 --> 01:05:45.680
and other livestock, as well as the red painted barns, are typical of the interior of BC.

01:05:45.680 --> 01:05:49.480
BC's farming wealth is dependent on irrigation.

01:05:49.480 --> 01:05:55.040
Much of the region is fairly arid, and only by tapping the mountain water supplies are

01:05:55.040 --> 01:05:59.160
farmers able to irrigate the fields and make the region productive.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:05.650
Like many places dependent on irrigation, few people ever consider the possibility of drought.

01:06:05.650 --> 01:06:11.810
But in recent years, B.C., like much of the Pacific Northwest, has suffered from little rain.

01:06:12.290 --> 01:06:21.010
And the limited water supplies have created predictable friction between the city populations and the farmers who are dependent on a regular supply.

01:06:25.290 --> 01:06:29.050
One of the region's oldest and largest industries is timber.

01:06:30.220 --> 01:06:44.580
Tall stands of pines and firs cover hundreds of thousands of acres in the central part of BC.

01:06:44.580 --> 01:06:49.110
The huge logging trucks can be seen throughout this part of the province.

01:06:49.110 --> 01:06:58.100
They are on their way to saw mills and pulp mills, which are the largest employers in central BC.

01:06:58.100 --> 01:07:00.000
Logging is controversial.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:03.070
No one would argue that lumber is, of course, necessary.

01:07:03.070 --> 01:07:08.550
In spite of all of today's modern materials, lumber is still used in great quantities to

01:07:08.550 --> 01:07:10.470
build our homes.

01:07:10.470 --> 01:07:15.220
The resource, unlike fossil fuels, is renewable.

01:07:15.220 --> 01:07:21.080
But how that resource is managed gives room for many different environmental views.

01:07:21.080 --> 01:07:23.610
Here the argument is over clear-cutting.

01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:26.890
It is still done, but in more limited degree.

01:07:26.890 --> 01:07:30.000
BC is trying to manage environmental concerns.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:32.780
against economic and human needs,

01:07:33.070 --> 01:07:37.850
and the dilemma inevitably leads to dissension between opposing sides.

01:07:38.930 --> 01:07:42.370
The pulp industry is one of the region's leading employers,

01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:45.590
but it is also a source of environmental concern.

01:07:46.920 --> 01:07:50.840
Canada is ahead of the U.S. in managing its ecological matters,

01:07:51.120 --> 01:07:54.950
but the further north you go, away from the border and the population centers,

01:07:54.950 --> 01:07:57.150
the more lax the standards become,

01:07:57.570 --> 01:08:00.000
and Canada is by no means without environmentalists.

01:08:00.030 --> 01:08:06.790
concerns. A short distance from Kamloops, the scenery changes. To the south, beautiful

01:08:06.790 --> 01:08:12.670
lakes offer activities for people from throughout the northwest. Through the region runs the

01:08:12.670 --> 01:08:20.190
Trans-Canada Highway, Highway #1. It stretches across the country. It is mostly two-lane.

01:08:20.190 --> 01:08:24.800
Canada has less four-lane cross-country highway development than the states.

01:08:30.190 --> 01:08:34.070
Kelowna lies 70 miles from Kamloops and is a lovely

01:08:34.070 --> 01:08:39.320
lake resort town. A modern outdoor sculpture stands beside the lake.

01:08:39.320 --> 01:08:42.680
It's the symbol of Kelowna and quite an impressive sight.

01:08:42.680 --> 01:08:48.510
This is vineyard country and the gentle slopes that rise up from the shore of the

01:08:48.510 --> 01:08:50.790
lake are covered with grape arbors

01:08:50.790 --> 01:08:54.190
and in the autumn the grapes will be harvested and processed

01:08:54.190 --> 01:09:00.000
in the wineries of BC. BC wines have become well known in recent years

01:09:00.220 --> 01:09:02.100
The grapes, because of the climate,

01:09:02.480 --> 01:09:05.360
produce wines of distinctive body and taste.

01:09:07.460 --> 01:09:10.120
To the west of Kamloops lies a part of B.C.

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:11.460
with a different appearance.

01:09:12.150 --> 01:09:15.190
Mountain streams give way to canyon country.

01:09:15.780 --> 01:09:18.380
Here, the Fraser River flows out of the mountains

01:09:18.380 --> 01:09:19.880
on its way to the sea.

01:09:20.770 --> 01:09:24.290
The Fraser River Canyon played a very important role

01:09:24.290 --> 01:09:26.330
in the history of Canadian trains.

01:09:27.140 --> 01:09:30.000
In addition to battling over which pass would be

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:31.220
used through the Rockies,

01:09:31.560 --> 01:09:33.580
the planners of the route also argued

01:09:33.580 --> 01:09:36.240
over which Pacific port would be best.

01:09:37.090 --> 01:09:39.610
B.C.'s only real settlement at the time

01:09:39.610 --> 01:09:41.330
was Victoria, out on the island.

01:09:41.880 --> 01:09:44.520
Vancouver as a city really didn't exist.

01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:48.180
The decision to terminate the line at Vancouver

01:09:48.180 --> 01:09:50.320
rather than several alternate ports

01:09:50.320 --> 01:09:52.200
was due to this river gorge,

01:09:52.200 --> 01:09:53.520
which, though not easy,

01:09:53.800 --> 01:09:56.000
was manageable for laying the tracks.

01:09:57.380 --> 01:10:00.000
The Canadian Pacific built most of its line

01:10:00.190 --> 01:10:05.900
on the south banks of the river by 1915 both the grand trunk and the canadian northern

01:10:05.900 --> 01:10:11.980
were building their lines mostly on the north side of the gorge today if you stand on the river's

01:10:11.980 --> 01:10:16.840
edge you may catch a glimpse of two different freights using the tracks at the same time

01:10:16.840 --> 01:10:24.080
these two main line tracks are among the busiest rail lines of the world dozens of freights move

01:10:24.080 --> 01:10:30.000
on opposite sides of the fraser river day and night hauling goods to and from the pacific coast

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:36.480
While the romance of passenger trains is appealing, the heart of the railroad is this:

01:10:36.480 --> 01:10:40.720
giant, lumbering freights that move goods coast to coast.

01:10:40.720 --> 01:10:44.990
Canadian freight trains are often 100 cars in length.

01:10:44.990 --> 01:10:48.040
They are among the longest freight trains in the world.

01:10:48.040 --> 01:10:55.280
The trains still provide the most efficient way to move goods to and from the deep water ports

01:10:55.280 --> 01:11:00.000
out over a sprawling nation of nearly 3,500 miles in Gloucester.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:05.910
with. A sign above the river marks the spot where the last spike of the Canadian Northern

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:11.530
was driven. It was the last spike of Canada's third transcontinental railroad. The first

01:11:11.530 --> 01:11:17.190
last spike was driven on the Canadian Pacific here at Kregalakee. This was the end of a

01:11:17.190 --> 01:11:23.380
tumultuous project. But unlike the Union Pacific, the railroad had been built in pieces. A section

01:11:23.380 --> 01:11:30.000
here, a section there, and the last spike was not a historic joining together of the

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:36.460
the last spike in a piecemeal system that had taken 14 years to complete. On November the 7th

01:11:36.460 --> 01:11:47.350
of 1885, the railroad was finished. It was the completion of a national dream. From Kamloops,

01:11:47.550 --> 01:11:53.220
the Canadian rolls through the Coast Mountains and into the Fraser River Canyon, making its way

01:11:53.220 --> 01:12:00.000
west on the final leg of the trip. When we arrive in Vancouver at 8 a.m., we will be complete

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:11.350
Leading a journey that has taken three days and three nights, covered 2,800 miles, crossed five provinces, the Great Lakes, the Prairies, the mountains, and the canyons of the West.

01:12:11.350 --> 01:12:16.420
This is one of the longest passenger rail journeys of the world.

01:12:53.320 --> 01:13:00.000
are now arriving in Canada's most important western port, Vancouver, a sparkling city surrounded by

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:06.420
water and mountains temperate and home to a million and a half people the Canadian approaches

01:13:06.420 --> 01:13:13.500
from the east on tracks that lead directly into Vancouver's main street station a great old railway

01:13:13.500 --> 01:13:21.820
station built in 1919 here the passengers leave the train we are at the end of the line people

01:13:21.820 --> 01:13:30.000
pass through the station under the central clock a fixture in any rail terminal and emerge in front

01:13:30.000 --> 01:14:00.000
of the town. Vancouver is Canada's great west coast city. It is a modern city. Almost everything is new. It began its explosive growth in only the past 30 years. It is Canada's link to the Far East and the population includes large groups of Asian immigrants. The Hotel Vancouver stands in the heart of town. It is one of Vancouver's largest hotels and one of the last of the great railway chateau hotels to be built.

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:09.720
It is impressive and gracious and still a center of activity in the heart of Vancouver.

01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:11.780
This is a world-class city.

01:14:11.780 --> 01:14:17.960
It boasts spectacular new architecture, a vibrant economic base, culture, recreation,

01:14:17.960 --> 01:14:23.580
a high standard of living, and compared to other cities of similar size, urban problems

01:14:23.580 --> 01:14:30.000
that are manageable and not out of hand, the population represents a mix of everything

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:36.280
canada has to offer immigrants from asia and europe and canadians who have moved here from

01:14:36.280 --> 01:14:43.670
the prairies and the east some for the mild weather others for culture or jobs and many for just the

01:14:43.670 --> 01:14:50.920
excitement a city like this provides the granville street bridge passes over an island almost in the

01:14:50.920 --> 01:14:58.750
center of town this is granville island for years it was purely industrial filled with warehouses

01:14:58.750 --> 01:14:59.960
and the shipping tray.

01:15:00.440 --> 01:15:09.060
Today, those businesses still exist, but developers have also created shops, markets, entertainment, and endless restaurants and cafes.

01:15:09.720 --> 01:15:12.590
This is one of Vancouver's most popular spots.

01:15:12.590 --> 01:15:26.810
It has become an active center for street entertainers and musicians, and many people of Vancouver will make their way here on Saturdays and Sundays to buy fresh food and fish at the public market on the pier.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:35.680
Vancouver began here in Gastown, a turn-of-the-century part of town that began to develop once the

01:15:35.680 --> 01:15:38.030
railroad arrived.

01:15:38.030 --> 01:15:42.900
In the center of Gastown is a historic town clock, the steam clock.

01:15:42.900 --> 01:15:48.790
It operates by, of all things, steam, city steam that is pumped under the streets as

01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:50.730
a public utility.

01:15:50.730 --> 01:15:56.500
The clock, pendulum, and weights are powered this way, and four times an hour, the chime

01:15:56.500 --> 01:15:59.920
sounds using wheezing old steam whistles.

01:16:00.090 --> 01:16:03.950
It's a curious attraction and very interesting to watch.

01:16:07.780 --> 01:16:12.440
Canada Place stands on the water next to the old Canadian Pacific Station.

01:16:13.180 --> 01:16:17.420
This is a sprawling new pier that was built for Vancouver's Expo

01:16:17.420 --> 01:16:22.540
and today acts as the terminal for the nearly two dozen different cruise ships

01:16:22.540 --> 01:16:26.880
that sail from Vancouver to Alaska during the summer months.

01:16:30.760 --> 01:16:35.150
The ships sail west through the harbor and under the Lions Gate Bridge.

01:16:35.150 --> 01:16:39.470
This suspension bridge is one of Vancouver's best-known sites.

01:16:39.470 --> 01:16:44.180
It links downtown with all of the communities on the North Shore.

01:16:44.180 --> 01:16:48.390
It is one of Vancouver's busiest thoroughfares.

01:16:48.390 --> 01:16:52.770
The road over the bridge passes through the center of Stanley Park.

01:16:52.770 --> 01:16:56.070
This is Vancouver's best-known public park.

01:16:56.070 --> 01:17:00.000
It is only minutes from downtown and is filled with people on

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:07.420
weekends enjoying the outdoors. There are marinas, bike paths, jogging trails, and lots of different

01:17:07.420 --> 01:17:14.890
things to do. The park's best-known attraction is a colorful group of totem poles. These brightly

01:17:14.890 --> 01:17:21.580
colored totems have stood in the park for a very long time. They are authentic and represent various

01:17:21.580 --> 01:17:30.000
Indian tribes scattered throughout B.C. The figures on these totem poles represent families, events,

01:17:30.060 --> 01:17:32.240
and the leaders of each clan.

01:17:32.240 --> 01:17:36.830
They do not, in most cases, represent gods or spirituality.

01:17:37.810 --> 01:17:42.570
They were carved to record the stories, the heritage, and the history of a people,

01:17:42.970 --> 01:17:46.280
not as idols of worship, which is commonly thought.

01:17:48.090 --> 01:17:50.250
The Canadian terminates in Vancouver,

01:17:50.610 --> 01:17:54.350
but Victoria, B.C.'s capital out on Vancouver Island,

01:17:54.570 --> 01:17:59.190
has a strong connection with Canada's original transcontinental trains.

01:18:00.510 --> 01:18:05.850
When British Columbia was being enticed into the newly formed Confederation, Victoria was

01:18:05.850 --> 01:18:11.240
the only settlement of any size. The planners of the railroad wanted to build the train to

01:18:11.240 --> 01:18:17.720
operate to Victoria by loading the train onto ferry boats, as is common in Europe. The plan

01:18:17.720 --> 01:18:22.740
was never carried out, and today Victoria remains unconnected to the main line of the train.

01:18:23.770 --> 01:18:30.000
Victoria today is Canada's most charming city. It is more English than London, and its sense of

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:36.250
grace and ease are world-renowned. This is the capital of British Columbia, and the legislature

01:18:36.250 --> 01:18:42.930
building on the water is famous for the Christmas-like outline of lights that glow across the harbor

01:18:42.930 --> 01:18:54.950
each night. The town is filled with buskers, street entertainers, tourists, and local people

01:18:54.950 --> 01:19:00.000
going about their day. Carriages move visitors through Victoria, and the old

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:05.820
The overall feeling is like that of being in an English seashore town.

01:19:05.820 --> 01:19:09.710
Victoria's best known gathering spot is the Empress Hotel.

01:19:09.710 --> 01:19:13.770
This is not just a hotel, this is a B.C. institution.

01:19:13.770 --> 01:19:18.070
It was built by the Canadian Pacific shortly after the turn of the century,

01:19:18.070 --> 01:19:23.710
anticipating that the train would eventually deliver passengers directly here.

01:19:23.710 --> 01:19:28.010
This never happened, but the hotel was an immediate success.

01:19:28.010 --> 01:19:29.860
The Empress's most famous

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:36.370
tea. Tea in the lobby of the empress at four o'clock goes back to the earliest days of the

01:19:36.370 --> 01:19:44.460
hotel. It reflects its English traditions and continues as popular as ever today. The quiet

01:19:44.460 --> 01:19:51.150
background sounds of clinking silver and animated conversation create an ambiance like that of the

01:19:51.150 --> 01:19:58.060
early trains, when travel was more leisurely and less raced, a time when travel was to be savored

01:19:58.060 --> 01:19:59.160
and enjoyed.

01:20:11.800 --> 01:20:14.700
From Victoria, we return by ferry to Vancouver

01:20:14.700 --> 01:20:18.020
and head to Union Station in the heart of downtown.

01:20:22.410 --> 01:20:25.890
At 9 o'clock tonight, the Canadian will depart to the east.

01:20:27.280 --> 01:20:30.000
The story of Canada and the story of the railroad

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:32.600
are in many respects one and the same.

01:20:32.600 --> 01:20:35.480
The building of the railroad united the nation.

01:20:35.960 --> 01:20:40.720
It created a national dream that was realized through hard work, tenacity,

01:20:41.240 --> 01:20:45.080
and the vision of optimistic men and women over a century ago.

01:20:45.990 --> 01:20:49.430
Pierre Burton, Canada's great writer and rail historian, wrote,

01:20:49.890 --> 01:20:55.150
We have little blood in our history, no searing civil war, no surgical revolution.

01:20:55.650 --> 01:21:00.000
We are the only nation in the world created non-violently by the building of the railroad.

01:21:00.060 --> 01:21:07.510
of a railroad. Today, the Canadian continues to travel both east and west across a great land.

01:21:07.930 --> 01:21:13.370
It speaks of the spirit of Canada, its people, and the noble link this nation has to its endless

01:21:13.370 --> 01:21:19.140
miles of silver rails. The Canadian is one of the last great passenger trains of the world,

01:21:19.540 --> 01:21:25.440
an experience enjoyed each year by thousands of devoted and enthusiastic travelers who still

01:21:25.440 --> 01:21:29.110
appreciate and understand the magic of a train.

01:22:06.420 --> 01:22:28.480
On the next world's greatest train ride video adventure.
